---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CriticalityDivider from '../../components/CriticalityDivider.astro';
import nvSetup from '../../assets/research/nv_setup.png';
import rawPl from '../../assets/research/nv/raw_pl_dc_removed.png';
import envelopeComparison from '../../assets/research/nv/envelope_comparison.png';
import quadratureEnvelope from '../../assets/research/nv/quadrature_envelope.png';
import lockinSweep from '../../assets/research/nv/lia_dispersive_sweep.png';
import sensFftShort from '../../assets/research/nv/sensitivity_fft_tc_short.png';
import sensFftMid from '../../assets/research/nv/sensitivity_fft_tc_mid.png';
import sensFftLong from '../../assets/research/nv/sensitivity_fft_tc_long.png';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="NV-center magnetometry"
  description="A room-temperature diamond magnetometer based on continuous-wave ODMR of nitrogen-vacancy ensembles, with frequency-modulated lock-in readout reaching about 10 nT/√Hz."
>
  <article class="shell">
    <p class="back"><a href={base + '/research'}>← Research</a></p>

    <header class="proj-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">2024–2026 · TÜBİTAK BİLGEM, Quantum Technologies</p>
      <h1>CW-ODMR and Lock-in Magnetometry with Nitrogen-Vacancy Centers</h1>
      <p class="proj-blurb">
        A diamond magnetometer built around continuous-wave optically detected magnetic
        resonance. Frequency modulation of the microwave drive converts the
        resonance dip into a dispersive lock-in signal that is linear in field at its zero
        crossing — giving a calibrated 36 µT/V response and a noise floor near 10 nT/√Hz.
      </p>

      <dl class="proj-meta">
        <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Summer intern, then part-time researcher</dd></div>
        <div>
          <dt>Topics</dt>
          <dd class="tags">
            <span class="tag">quantum sensing</span>
            <span class="tag">ODMR</span>
            <span class="tag">lock-in detection</span>
            <span class="tag">spin Hamiltonian</span>
            <span class="tag">magnetometry</span>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Materials</dt>
          <dd class="links">
            <a href="https://github.com/borankilic/NV_center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code (GitHub) ↗</a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The sensor</h2>
      <p>
        The negatively charged nitrogen-vacancy center is a point defect — a substitutional
        nitrogen next to a lattice vacancy — whose ground state is a spin-1 triplet with a
        zero-field splitting <em>D</em> ≈ 2.87 GHz between <em>m</em><sub>s</sub> = 0 and
        <em>m</em><sub>s</sub> = ±1. Including the Zeeman term and the hyperfine coupling to
        the host <sup>14</sup>N nucleus (<em>I</em> = 1), the ground-state spin Hamiltonian is
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>H</em>/<em>h</em> = <em>D S</em><sub><em>z</em></sub><sup>2</sup>
        + <em>γ</em><sub>e</sub> <strong>B</strong>·<strong>S</strong>
        + <strong>S</strong>·<em>A</em>·<strong>I</strong>
      </div>

      <p>
        with <em>γ</em><sub>e</sub> = <em>g</em><sub>e</sub><em>μ</em><sub>B</sub>/<em>h</em>
        ≈ 28.02 GHz/T. To first order the Zeeman interaction splits the
        <em>m</em><sub>s</sub> = ±1 levels symmetrically about <em>D</em>, so the two resonances
        separate by
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        Δ<em>f</em> = 2<em>γ</em><sub>e</sub><em>B</em><sub>∥</sub>
      </div>

      <p>
        where <em>B</em><sub>∥</sub> is the field projected on the NV symmetry axis — this is the
        quantity the whole instrument is built to extract. The hyperfine term splits each
        resonance further into three lines separated by <em>A</em><sub>∥</sub> ≈ 2.16 MHz, one
        for each projection of the <sup>14</sup>N nuclear spin, <em>m</em><sub>I</sub> = −1, 0,
        +1. Green 532 nm light both polarises the spin into <em>m</em><sub>s</sub> = 0 and reads
        it out through spin-dependent intersystem crossing: when a swept microwave tone hits a
        transition, population leaves the bright <em>m</em><sub>s</sub> = 0 state and the
        637–800 nm photoluminescence drops.
      </p>

      <h2>The instrument</h2>
      <p>
        The optical path is a 532 nm CW laser split at a beam splitter, with one arm sent to a
        monitor photodiode for intensity referencing and the other focused onto the diamond
        through a 10.6 mm focal-length aspheric lens. The same lens collects the returned
        fluorescence, which a dichroic mirror separates from the excitation path and a long-pass
        filter cleans of residual 532 nm scatter before it lands on two Thorlabs PDA100A-EC
        photodetectors. On the microwave side, an Anritsu MG3692C generator supplies the drive
        tone, a Mini-Circuits ZRL-3500+ amplifier brings it to useful power, and a loop antenna
        placed near the diamond couples it into the sample. A pair of Helmholtz coils provides
        controlled bias and test fields. Demodulation runs on a Stanford Research SR860 lock-in
        amplifier referenced to the microwave modulation, and both the raw photodetector signal
        and the lock-in output are digitized on a TiePie Handyscope HS5.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={nvSetup} alt="Block diagram of the magnetometer: 532 nm laser, beam splitter, dichroic mirror, aspheric lens onto the diamond, long-pass filter and photodetectors; and the electronic chain with RF generator, amplifier, Helmholtz coils and lock-in amplifier." widths={[600, 1000, 1500]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          The instrument. <strong>Optical:</strong> a 532 nm pump focused by a 10.6 mm aspheric
          lens; fluorescence in the 637–800 nm band is collected by the same lens, separated by
          a dichroic, cleaned by a long-pass filter and detected on two PDA100A-EC photodiodes.
          <strong>Electronic:</strong> an MG3692C microwave source, amplified by a ZRL-3500+ into
          a loop antenna, with Helmholtz coils for field control and an SR860 lock-in amplifier
          referenced to the modulation, logged on a Handyscope HS5.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Resolving the hyperfine structure</h2>
      <p>
        Diamond's host lattice gives NV centers four possible orientations, one along each of
        the four ⟨111⟩ crystallographic axes. An arbitrarily directed bias field projects
        differently onto each axis, so an ensemble containing all four orientations shows four
        Zeeman-split pairs, not one, each shifted by its own <em>B</em><sub>∥</sub>.
      </p>

      <p>
        To resolve this directly I stacked roughly ten neodymium bar magnets on the XYZ stage to
        produce a strong bias field — deliberately not an ideal or well-controlled one. The
        field's direction and magnitude at the diamond are not known precisely: some field lines
        pass through the metal of the stage, and a bar magnet's field is highly inhomogeneous. A
        high-turn Helmholtz coil would be the better source, but the coils available could not be
        driven to a comparably high field. Sweeping 2.874 GHz ± 10 MHz — frequency modulation on
        the RF generator with a 10 MHz deviation, a 0.1 Hz triangular rate, and the output kept at
        or below 10 dBm — and running the same envelope-recovery pipeline described below
        resolved eight sets of three peaks. Each set of three is the <em>m</em><sub>I</sub> = −1,
        0, +1 <sup>14</sup>N hyperfine triplet; the whole pattern is symmetric about 2.874 GHz
        because each orientation's levels are already split into <em>m</em><sub>s</sub> = −1 and
        <em>m</em><sub>s</sub> = +1 branches by the (arbitrarily directed) bias field; and there
        are four distinct groups, not one, because the four crystallographic orientations each see
        a different projection of that field. Four orientations times two Zeeman branches gives
        the eight sets of three actually observed.
      </p>

      <p>
        This spectrum is a useful diagnostic in its own right: the four splittings encode the
        bias field's direction relative to the diamond lattice, and resolving all eight sets
        confirms both adequate microwave coupling bandwidth and narrow enough linewidths across
        the span. It is also the calibration step for everything that follows — operating the
        magnetometer means picking one line and working the lock-in scheme around it, so knowing
        exactly where the lines sit under a given bias is a prerequisite, not a side measurement.
      </p>

      <h2>Getting a usable signal out of noise</h2>
      <p>
        Before demodulation is trusted to do the heavy lifting, it is worth looking at what the
        raw photodetector signal looks like: a slowly drifting DC level from laser intensity
        fluctuations, beam alignment, and thermal drift, with the ODMR information riding on top
        as a much smaller AC-coupled feature. Fitting a Lorentzian directly to the raw trace is
        fragile for exactly this reason — the fit competes against drift that is often comparable
        in size to the signal itself.
      </p>

      <p>
        Removing the DC component isolates the AC content, but the result is still noisy enough
        that its envelope — the quantity that actually carries the resonance shape — needs
        careful recovery. I compared five approaches on the same records: moving-average and
        low-pass filtering, upper-envelope detection, peak detection, an exponential follower,
        and a quadrature (Hilbert-transform-like) envelope that reconstructs the analytic signal
        and takes its magnitude. The quadrature envelope recovered the peak structure most
        faithfully — it does not lag like a causal exponential follower, and it does not depend
        on locating discrete peaks, so it degrades gracefully as contrast drops. Separately,
        reconstructing the spectrum from only the above-threshold components of its Fourier
        transform suppresses narrowband electromagnetic interference from nearby lab electronics
        without touching the broadband resonance feature.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={rawPl} alt="Raw photodetector output whose axis is labeled as time but is actually swept microwave frequency, showing a constant DC offset, and the same record with that offset subtracted." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Raw photodetector output (top) — the axis reads as time but is actually the swept
          microwave frequency — riding on a constant 4.974638 V DC offset, and the same record
          with that offset subtracted (bottom), isolating the AC content that carries the
          resonance information.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={envelopeComparison} alt="Comparison of envelope-recovery methods — moving average, upper envelope, peak detection, exponential follower, and quadrature envelope — applied to the same noisy record." widths={[600, 1000, 1268]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Five envelope-recovery methods on the same noisy record: moving average, upper
          envelope, peak detection, exponential follower, and the quadrature envelope. The
          quadrature method tracks the true peak structure most closely without lagging or
          relying on discrete peak identification.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={quadratureEnvelope} alt="The quadrature envelope overlaid on the raw AC signal, showing it closely follows the underlying resonance shape." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          The quadrature envelope (bold) overlaid on the raw AC signal it was extracted from —
          the method used downstream wherever an envelope, rather than the raw oscillation, is
          the quantity of interest.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <CriticalityDivider label="lock-in readout" />

      <h2>Lock-in detection</h2>
      <p>
        Sweeping the microwave frequency and fitting the resulting dip is still too slow for
        real-time field tracking, and the dip sits on the same drifting background discussed
        above. A single ODMR feature is
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>F</em>(<em>ω</em>) = <em>F</em><sub>0</sub>
        <span class="brk">[</span>1 −
        <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>C</em></span><span class="den">1 + 4(<em>ω</em>−<em>ω</em><sub>0</sub>)<sup>2</sup>/<em>ν</em><sup>2</sup></span></span>
        <span class="brk">]</span>
      </div>

      <p>
        with contrast <em>C</em> and FWHM <em>ν</em> ≈ 18.3 MHz for our ensemble. Instead of
        sweeping and fitting, the carrier is frequency modulated at <em>f</em><sub>mod</sub> =
        18 kHz (square-wave, in the derivation that follows, which samples the lineshape at
        exactly two points per cycle — though a companion electronics log for this measurement
        instead records the modulation as a sine at the same rate and deviation, so treat the
        exact waveform as not fully settled between the two reports),
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>ω</em><sub>MW</sub>(<em>t</em>) = <em>ω</em><sub>C</sub>
        + <em>ω</em><sub>dev</sub> · square(2π<em>f</em><sub>mod</sub><em>t</em>),
        <span class="eq-where">f<sub>mod</sub> = 18 kHz</span>
      </div>

      <p>
        which moves the measurement band far above the 1/<em>f</em> corner set by laser and
        thermal drift. Demodulating at <em>f</em><sub>mod</sub> returns the <em>difference</em>
        of the lineshape sampled at <em>ω</em><sub>C</sub> ± <em>ω</em><sub>dev</sub> — a
        dispersive curve that crosses zero exactly at <em>ω</em><sub>C</sub> = <em>ω</em><sub>0</sub>.
        Hold the carrier there and any applied field shifts <em>ω</em><sub>0</sub>, which shows up
        as a lock-in output proportional to the field, with no fitting required. Sensitivity is
        set by the slope at that zero crossing, and differentiating the idealized
        single-Lorentzian lineshape above with respect to <em>ω</em><sub>dev</sub> shows that
        slope is steepest at a specific modulation depth:
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>ω</em><sub>dev</sub> = <span class="frac"><span class="num"><em>ν</em></span><span class="den">2√3</span></span>
      </div>

      <p>
        which for <em>ν</em> = 18.3 MHz works out to <em>ω</em><sub>dev</sub> ≈ 5.3 MHz. In
        practice the deviation used for magnetometry was found empirically — sweeping
        <em>ω</em><sub>dev</sub> and directly maximising the measured zero-crossing slope — and
        settled at <em>ω</em><sub>dev</sub> ≈ 1.2 MHz, well below that theoretical value. The gap
        is a real limitation of the single-Lorentzian model: the actual resonance is split by the
        <sup>14</sup>N hyperfine coupling into three overlapping lines, and the clean optimum
        above does not account for that structure. Operated at the empirical working point, the
        lock-in output is linear in applied field over the useful range spanning roughly the
        linewidth around <em>ω</em><sub>0</sub>. Sweeping the carrier through resonance and
        recording the lock-in output directly gives that dispersive curve;
        a linear fit through its zero crossing gives the slope, and its inverse — calibrated
        against a fluxgate reference — is the voltage-to-field conversion factor
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>κ</em> = <em>γ</em><sub><em>B</em></sub><sup>−1</sup> = 36 µT/V
      </div>

      <p>
        From here the magnetometer's output is simply <em>κ</em> times the lock-in voltage — no
        further spectroscopy needed until the carrier drifts out of the linear region.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={lockinSweep} alt="Lock-in ODMR spectrum from a frequency sweep: the dispersive lineshape with smoothed data and a linear fit through the zero crossing." widths={[600, 1000, 1226]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Lock-in output versus carrier frequency across a single resonance: the dispersive
          curve (smoothed) with a linear fit through its zero crossing. The fitted slope, via
          the fluxgate calibration, sets <em>κ</em> = 36 µT/V.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Sensitivity and the bandwidth trade-off</h2>
      <p>
        With no test field applied, the lock-in X-channel trace is converted to field units,
        <em>B</em><sub>meas</sub>(<em>t</em>) = <em>κ V</em><sub>LIA</sub>(<em>t</em>), and the
        sensitivity follows the standard definition
      </p>

      <div class="eq">
        <em>η</em> = <em>σ</em><sub><em>B</em></sub> ⁄ √(2<em>f</em><sub>ENBW</sub>)
      </div>

      <p>
        where <em>f</em><sub>ENBW</sub> is the equivalent noise bandwidth of the lock-in
        low-pass filter. Equivalently, the FFT of <em>B</em><sub>meas</sub>(<em>t</em>) gives the
        noise floor as a function of frequency directly, and it is instructive to look at that
        floor at several time constants rather than quote a single number. At a short time
        constant the cutoff is high and the floor only rolls off around 1–2 kHz; at an
        intermediate constant the roll-off moves down to roughly 200 Hz; at a long constant it
        moves down further, to around 20 Hz. In every case the floor below the roll-off is set
        by the same low-frequency technical noise — laser intensity fluctuations and slow
        thermal drift — that the modulation scheme was designed to move away from, and narrowing
        the lock-in bandwidth does not remove that noise, only excludes more of the higher band
        where it is already smaller.
      </p>

      <p>
        With a 3 ms time constant the measured sensitivity is roughly
        <strong>100 nT/√Hz in the 10–100 Hz band</strong>, improving to about
        <strong>10 nT/√Hz above 200 Hz</strong> where the technical noise has mostly rolled off.
        The time constant is therefore a genuine trade-off, not a free parameter: a longer
        constant quiets the output but cuts bandwidth, directly limiting how fast a real field
        change can be tracked.
      </p>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={sensFftShort} alt="Log-log plot of sensitivity in nT per root Hz versus frequency at a short lock-in time constant, with the noise floor rolling off around 1-2 kHz." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Sensitivity spectrum at a short lock-in time constant: wide bandwidth, roll-off near
          1–2 kHz.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={sensFftMid} alt="Log-log plot of sensitivity in nT per root Hz versus frequency at an intermediate lock-in time constant, with the noise floor rolling off around 200 Hz." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          Same measurement at an intermediate time constant: the roll-off moves down to roughly
          200 Hz as the low-pass narrows.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <figure class="fig">
        <Image src={sensFftLong} alt="Log-log plot of sensitivity in nT per root Hz versus frequency at a long lock-in time constant, with the noise floor rolling off around 20 Hz." widths={[600, 1000, 1400]} sizes="(max-width: 44rem) 92vw, 42rem" />
        <figcaption>
          At a long time constant the roll-off moves down further, to near 20 Hz — the
          bandwidth/noise trade-off set entirely by the lock-in filter.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Conclusions</h2>
      <p>
        The instrument does what it was built to do: a swept spectrum under bias resolves eight
        sets of three hyperfine lines from the four NV orientations, confirming the spin physics
        and giving a clean calibration point, and the frequency-modulated lock-in scheme turns
        that spectroscopy into a continuous, linear field readout with a measured 36 µT/V conversion
        and a sensitivity of order 100 nT/√Hz at low frequency, improving toward 10 nT/√Hz above
        a few hundred hertz. The dominant limitation right now is not the spin physics or the
        detection electronics — it is technical noise entering through the optical path, chiefly
        laser intensity fluctuations and slow thermal drift, which sets the floor at low
        frequency regardless of how the lock-in time constant is tuned.
      </p>

      <p>
        The most direct fix is <strong>balanced detection</strong>: splitting the collected
        fluorescence onto two photodiodes and subtracting, so laser-intensity noise common to
        both arms cancels while the differential ODMR signal survives. Separately, since the
        <sup>14</sup>N hyperfine splitting divides the available contrast across three lines
        instead of concentrating it in one, <strong>hyperfine-driven multi-tone excitation</strong> —
        driving all three at once — should recover contrast the current single-tone scheme
        discards. And because the linear range around the zero crossing is set by the resonance
        linewidth, <strong>closed-loop PI tracking</strong> of that crossing would let the carrier
        follow the resonance rather than sit at a fixed point, extending the dynamic range well
        beyond a static carrier. An application of this platform — NV-ensemble magnetic anomaly
        detection — was presented as a poster at <strong>KOBIT 2025</strong>, and is the more
        immediate motivation for pushing sensitivity and dynamic range further.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class="proj-foot">
      <a href={base + '/research'}>← Back to research</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</Base>

